          title: "“Which is easier?” — the invisible claim backed by the visible one",
          body: "Four people carry a fifth to Jesus, and he responds to something nobody mentioned: sins. The scribes' silent objection is *correct theology* — forgiving sins is God's business, and announcing it on God's behalf without altar or sacrifice is precisely what blasphemy meant. Jesus answers with a piece of logic worth slowing down for. *Anyone* can say \"your sins are forgiven\" — the claim is unfalsifiable, which makes it cheap. \"Rise and walk\" is checkable within seconds. So he stakes the unverifiable claim on the verifiable one: *so that you may know* the Son of Man has authority **on earth** to forgive — then the man stands up. It is as close as the gospels come to an offered proof, and note what it is a proof *of*: not his power (v. 8 says the crowd glorified God for giving such authority *to men*) but his right to say the one sentence every guilty conscience most doubts it may hear. One more detail: he saw **their** faith — the friends'. The paralysed man never speaks and is never said to believe anything. Sometimes, this verse quietly allows, faith is four people carrying a fifth.",
