      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a9.9.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g9.9.11', 'g9.9.12'],
          lemma: "τελώνιον … Μαθθαῖον λεγόμενον",
          title: "One verse of autobiography",
          body: "The τελώνιον was a toll station on the trade road through Capernaum, and the man sitting at it belonged to the most despised professional class in Jewish Palestine: tax farmers bought the right to collect and profited from the margin, which made them by definition extortioners, and their daily work for the occupier made them collaborators — hence the fixed phrase this chapter uses twice, *tax collectors and sinners*, one social category. Mark and Luke tell this same story about a man named **Levi**; only this gospel names him **Matthew** — the name of the apostle this gospel has been credited to since the second century. The book itself is anonymous, and honesty requires saying that scholars are divided about the tradition. But if it is right, then this verse is the author's entire autobiography: nine Greek words locating himself at the toll booth, the worst line on any first-century résumé, with no explanation offered and none of the self-justifying detail a fabricator would add. Two words from Jesus — *follow me* — and one participle of response: ἀναστάς, **rising**, the same verb this gospel will use of resurrection. Whoever wrote it, the story is told the way a man tells the story of the day his life split in two.",
        },
      ],
